const e=[{community_id:0,name:"Alignment, Safety & Interpretability",tagline:"Opening the black box to control it",description:"These papers treat safety and interpretability as two faces of one problem: understanding what happens inside a trained model well enough to govern it. One thread hardens language models against jailbreaks and harmful fine-tuning, arguing that alignment reaching only a few tokens deep is brittle and proposing backtracking, perturbation attenuation, and deeper safety objectives. A second opens the model with mechanistic tools: sparse autoencoders, sparse feature circuits, attention-head analysis, and the geometry of learned concepts. A third quantifies provenance and responsibility through influence functions, Data Shapley, and unlearning, extending attribution even to diffusion models and copyright-protected generation. Benchmarks like DarkBench probe manipulative behavior. The unifying move is to make model internals legible, and therefore accountable.",methods:["Sparse autoencoders and feature circuits","Influence functions and Data Shapley attribution","Safety-alignment fine-tuning and defenses","Attention-head and concept-geometry analysis","Machine unlearning and knowledge editing"],key_contribution:"Safety follows from interpretability: exposing a model's internal mechanisms, data provenance, and failure modes is the precondition for governing its behavior.",exemplars:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708"},{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946"},{ay:"Choi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08069"},{ay:"Mlodozeniec 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13850"},{ay:"Kran 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.10728"}]},{community_id:1,name:"Foundation Models / LLMs",tagline:"Engineering capability from data to reasoning",description:"This community pursues the frontier-model lifecycle from pretraining data through post-training reasoning. On the input side, papers formalize data selection as optimal control, generate synthetic corpora for continued pretraining, and decouple embeddings for multi-domain training. On the architecture and efficiency side, mixture-of-experts designs add zero-computation experts, modular decomposition compresses models, and large-vocabulary losses are made memory-cheap. A prominent reasoning thread teaches models to self-improve, self-correct via reinforcement learning, and grow into reasoning generalists, exemplified by WizardMath's reinforced instruction evolution, which has become widely cited. A skeptical strand exposes evaluation confounds, from training on the test task to benchmark cheating by null models, warning that measured emergence can be an artifact. Together they ask how far better data, sparsity, and self-generated supervision can push capability.",methods:["Mixture-of-experts and model compression","Pretraining data selection and synthetic data","Reinforcement learning for self-improvement","Test-time compute and speculative decoding","Benchmark-confound and evaluation analysis"],key_contribution:"Frontier capability is engineered end-to-end through curated and synthetic data, sparse efficient architectures, and self-generated supervision, while headline benchmarks are treated with earned suspicion.",exemplars:[{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Luo 2023",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2308.09583"},{ay:"Yang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.07431"},{ay:"Gu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07064"},{ay:"PENG 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.02108"}]},{community_id:2,name:"Graphs, Probabilistic & Other Methods",tagline:"Guarantees for graphs, sequences, and distributions",description:"A methods-heavy cluster united less by application than by shared mathematical machinery for structure and uncertainty. One core is sequence modeling beyond attention: linear RNNs and oscillatory state-space models whose spectral properties, from negative eigenvalues to forced harmonic oscillators, govern long-range state tracking. A second is graph learning theory, characterizing expressivity through graph homomorphism, Hölder stability of message passing, spectral rewiring, and logical descriptions of what GNNs can distinguish. A third is probabilistic inference: Gaussian processes on manifolds, high-dimensional Bayesian optimization, variational diffusion posterior sampling, and Feynman-Kac control for irregular time series. Causal discovery under selection and intervention rounds it out. The shared move is to derive guarantees, on expressivity, stability, or convergence, for models handling graphs, dynamics, and distributions.",methods:["Linear RNNs and state-space models","Message-passing GNN expressivity analysis","Gaussian processes and Bayesian optimization","Variational inference and posterior sampling","Causal discovery and spectral methods"],key_contribution:"Progress comes from principled analysis of the underlying machinery, spectral, geometric, and probabilistic, that lets models represent graphs, sequences, and distributions with provable guarantees.",exemplars:[{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537"},{ay:"Rusch 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.03943"},{ay:"MOUFAD 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09945"},{ay:"Gai 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00485"},{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.16032"}]},{community_id:3,name:"Science, Robotics & Systems",tagline:"Machine learning grounded in physical structure",description:"Where machine learning meets the physical and biological world, this cluster builds domain-grounded models across science, robotics, and systems. Structural biology is central: flow- and diffusion-based generators design protein backbones and families, dock molecules, and diffuse shape and electrostatics for drug design. Physics and simulation appear as neural PDE solvers, diffusion graph networks for fluids, and Gaussian-splatting training systems. Neuroscience contributes topographic language models, neural-population geometry, and cortical vision models. Robotics studies data scaling laws for imitation learning, in-context policies via graph diffusion, and diffusion planning for autonomous driving. KAN revisits network primitives themselves through the Kolmogorov-Arnold theorem, a paper that has become broadly influential. The through-line is adapting flow matching, diffusion, and geometric structure to hard-won scientific priors.",methods:["Flow matching and diffusion for structure generation","Imitation learning and diffusion planning","Graph and neural-field PDE simulation","Optimal transport for dynamics","Geometric and equivariant modeling"],key_contribution:"Scientific and robotic progress comes from embedding domain structure, molecular geometry, physical dynamics, neural organization, into flow-, diffusion-, and geometry-based learners.",exemplars:[{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710"},{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.19756"},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18647"},{ay:"Valencia 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.02843"},{ay:"Rathi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11516"}]},{community_id:4,name:"Multimodal Applications",tagline:"Controllable perception on pretrained backbones",description:"This cluster applies generative and foundation models to perception across vision, video, audio, and 3D. Controllable video generation is a hub: adapter frameworks route diverse controls into diffusion backbones, and audio-driven models animate portrait avatars and talking bodies with long-term motion coherence. A 3D thread spans transformer view synthesis, pose-free Gaussian splatting, volumetric-mesh geometry, and structured neural-field reconstruction. Promptable segmentation, led by SAM 2 for images and video alongside real-time and 3D variants, anchors open-vocabulary understanding and has been widely adopted. Multimodal LLMs extend to GUI-agent visual grounding, chart reasoning, and spatio-temporal video grounding. The shared move is engineering controllability, real-time efficiency, and 3D consistency into large pretrained backbones so they operate reliably on real images, scenes, and interfaces.",methods:["Controllable diffusion with control adapters","Audio-conditioned video generation","3D Gaussian splatting and neural fields","Promptable segmentation (SAM-style)","Multimodal LLM visual grounding"],key_contribution:"Perceptual applications advance by bolting controllability, 3D consistency, and real-time efficiency onto large pretrained diffusion and multimodal backbones.",exemplars:[{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.09967"},{ay:"Jiang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.02634"},{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.17242"},{ay:"Ravi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.00714"},{ay:"Gou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.05243"}]},{community_id:5,name:"Generative Models",tagline:"Faster, more general diffusion and flow",description:"The generative-models cluster concentrates on the mathematics and efficiency of diffusion and flow matching. A dominant concern is fast, high-fidelity sampling: continuous-time consistency models, one-step shortcut models, learned ODE discretization, and optimal covariance matching all cut the number of denoising steps. A unifying-theory thread generalizes generation to arbitrary Markov processes via generator matching, to discrete paths via kinetic-optimal flow matching, and to finite symmetric groups. Autoregressive visual generation is rethought through learned video tokenizers, the vector-quantization rotation trick, and guidance-free condition contrastive alignment, while block diffusion interpolates between autoregressive and diffusion language models. Representation alignment shows diffusion transformers train faster when their features match self-supervised encoders. The common aim is faster, more general, more controllable samplers built on principled foundations.",methods:["Consistency and distillation for fast sampling","Flow matching and generator matching","Discrete and autoregressive diffusion","Vector quantization and tokenization","Representation alignment for diffusion transformers"],key_contribution:"Generative modeling advances by unifying diffusion and flow matching under general stochastic-process theory while collapsing sampling to a few, or one, efficient steps.",exemplars:[{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21264"},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940"},{ay:"Lu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11081"},{ay:"Frans 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.12557"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null}]},{community_id:6,name:"Learning Theory & Optimization",tagline:"Turning observed behavior into proofs",description:"This theory cluster seeks provable explanations for phenomena that deep learning discovered empirically. Optimization work delivers convergence and complexity results: min-max methods with lazy Hessians, tight lower bounds under high-order smoothness, convex duality for regularized-network loss landscapes, and fast gradient methods for time-varying and polymatrix games. A generalization strand proves neural collapse under weight decay, characterizes single-index feature learning, and asks when task-vector model editing provably works. A distinctively modern strand analyzes LLM behavior formally: transformers solving parity efficiently with chain of thought, self-improvement as a statistical sharpening mechanism, and hard limits on scalable LLM-as-judge evaluation. Optimizer design, from transformation-invariant LoRA to residual-path gradient propagation, links theory to practice. The unifying move is turning observed behavior into guarantees.",methods:["Convergence and complexity analysis","Convex duality and loss-landscape theory","Min-max and game-theoretic optimization","Generalization and feature-learning bounds","Neural tangent kernel analysis"],key_contribution:"The school converts empirically observed behavior, neural collapse, chain-of-thought reasoning, self-improvement, into provable optimization and generalization guarantees.",exemplars:[{ay:"Fagnou 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2501.17086"},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Kim 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08633"},{ay:"Jacot 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.04887"},{ay:"Hollender 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.07398"}]},{community_id:7,name:"Datasets & Benchmarks",tagline:"Contamination-resistant yardsticks for real capability",description:"This cluster builds the measuring instruments for modern AI, arguing that capability claims are only as trustworthy as the evaluations behind them. Benchmarks target LLM sub-skills specifically: reward-model discrimination with RM-Bench, realistic code generation with tool use in BigCodeBench, personalization in PrefEval, and multi-table multi-hop question answering. Agentic evaluation raises the stakes with machine-learning-engineering competitions in MLE-bench and cybersecurity tasks in Cybench. Multimodal evaluation stresses interleaved comprehension, physical-world understanding, and dynamically varied complexity to resist contamination. A methodological strand confronts dataset bias, similarity-aware generalization, and forecaster consistency, while domain benchmarks reach theorem proving, enterprise text-to-SQL, materials charge density, and pathology. The through-line is contamination-resistant, capability-specific measurement that exposes where models, especially as agents, actually fail.",methods:["Capability-specific benchmark construction","Agentic task evaluation","Contamination-resistant dynamic evaluation","Reward-model and judge assessment","Multi-agent dataset generation"],key_contribution:"Trustworthy progress requires contamination-resistant, capability-specific benchmarks, especially for agents, that reveal concrete failure modes rather than headline scores.",exemplars:[{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.16184"},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877"},{ay:"Xia 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.10139"},{ay:"Chan 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07095"},{ay:"Zhao 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.09597"}]},{community_id:8,name:"Reinforcement Learning",tagline:"Toward broadly generalizing, sample-efficient agents",description:"The reinforcement-learning cluster works toward agents that generalize across tasks and environments rather than mastering just one. Open-ended and generalist directions dominate: physics-based task generation for training general agents in Kinetix, and retrieval-augmented in-context agents in REGENT. Skill discovery is reframed through mutual-information objectives and AI-feedback skill design in MaestroMotif, while model-based methods improve sample efficiency via prioritized generative replay and long-short-term imagination. Alignment is cast as a game, with iterative Nash policy optimization over general preferences beyond Bradley-Terry rewards. A theory strand studies the geometry of continuous-control RL, flat-minima robustness, and efficient learning under linear Bellman completeness, and planning appears as learned options and search from demonstrations. The shared aim is broadly capable, sample-efficient decision-makers grounded in principled objectives.",methods:["Open-ended environment and curriculum design","Unsupervised and hierarchical skill learning","Model-based RL and generative replay","Preference and game-theoretic policy optimization","Planning and learned search"],key_contribution:"The school pushes from single-task mastery toward sample-efficient, broadly generalizing agents built on principled skill, preference, and planning objectives.",exemplars:[{ay:"Matthews 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.23208"},{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617"},{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18082"},{ay:"Klissarov 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.08542"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.08021"}]},{community_id:9,name:"Representation & Transfer Learning",tagline:"Understanding representations to transfer them",description:"This cluster asks what representations models actually learn and how to transfer them without forgetting. A theory strand pursues identifiability, proving cross-entropy inverts the data-generating process, identifying temporal causal representations with instantaneous dependence, and explaining why FixMatch generalizes better than supervised learning. A continual-learning strand mitigates catastrophic forgetting with function vectors and decoupled low-rank adaptation for class-incremental settings. Vision-language representation is scrutinized through hyperbolic entailment embeddings and the modality gap and object bias in CLIP-style contrastive models. Domain adaptation appears as source-free proxy denoising and LiDAR test-time adaptation. Neuroscience-inspired work, from Kuramoto oscillatory neurons to conformally isometric grid cells and adaptive dynamic computation, probes how structure emerges in learned features. The unifying move is understanding representation geometry to make transfer principled and forgetting-resistant.",methods:["Identifiability and causal representation learning","Low-rank adaptation and function vectors","Contrastive vision-language representation","Source-free and test-time domain adaptation","Feature-learning generalization analysis"],key_contribution:"Robust transfer and continual learning follow from understanding the geometry and identifiability of the representations models actually learn.",exemplars:[{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.11019"},{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11206"},{ay:"Pal 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06912"},{ay:"Reizinger 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21869"},{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2405.15325"}]}],i={title:"Benchmarks That Never Ask What a Representation Is",statement:"The two schools that barely share references are Datasets & Benchmarks and Representation & Transfer Learning. The benchmark cluster builds evaluation instruments for behavior at the surface, reward models, code generation, multimodal comprehension, ML-engineering and cybersecurity agents, while the representation cluster asks what internal structure makes a model transfer, forget, or remain identifiable. The missing bridge is a shared instrument: an evaluation that scores representation quality, transferability, or identifiability directly, rather than treating the model as a black box that either passes or fails a task. As a result, the field measures what models do without measuring what they have learned.",structural_evidence:"In the 10-community coupling adjacency, Datasets & Benchmarks and Representation & Transfer Learning have the lowest inter-cluster weight of any pair (6.6), meaning their papers almost never cite the same prior work. The contrast is stark: the strongest pair, Alignment, Safety & Interpretability with Foundation Models / LLMs, is coupled at 404.4, roughly sixty times heavier. Even Datasets & Benchmarks' own tie to Foundation Models (125.1) dwarfs its link to representation research.",why_it_matters:"Benchmarks increasingly drive what the community optimizes, so a benchmark blind to representation structure steers effort toward models that score well while learning brittle or entangled features. Meanwhile representation theory that never touches a shared benchmark stays unfalsified and hard to adopt. Closing the gap would let claims about transfer, forgetting, and identifiability be tested on the same agentic and multimodal tasks that now define progress.",what_would_resolve_it:"A diagnostic benchmark that scores foundation models on representation-level properties, transfer efficiency, catastrophic-forgetting resistance, modality-gap size, and identifiability, would force the two clusters to share references. Zhang 2025's similarity-aware evaluation of drug-target prediction and Liu 2025's A Decade's Battle on Dataset Bias already gesture at representation-aware evaluation; extending that instrumentation to the settings studied by Reizinger 2024 (identifiability) and Jiang 2025 (function vectors for forgetting) would build the bridge directly."},a={claim:"Across these 213 Orals, the collective argument is that the 2025 frontier is shifting from brute-force parameter scaling to the deliberate structuring and allocation of computation, data, and representation, reallocating effort across training, inference, and evaluation rather than simply making models bigger.",evidence:[{point:"A large share of the cohort buys capability through architectural and training structure rather than raw size, heterogeneous expert routing, modular decomposition, and decoupled embeddings all extract more from a fixed budget.",papers:[{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.09632"},{ay:"Iacob 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.05021"}]},{point:"Data is treated as something to be curated and selected, not merely accumulated, with synthetic augmentation, optimal-control selection, and submodular file selection standing in for more tokens.",papers:[{ay:"Yang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.07431"},{ay:"Gu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07064"},{ay:"Fan 2025",doi:null}]},{point:"Inference-time compute emerges as a first-class axis of capability, spending more at test time on reasoning or retrieval, and compressing diffusion sampling into one or a few steps, rather than enlarging the model.",papers:[{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Yue 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.04343"},{ay:"Frans 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.12557"},{ay:"Lu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11081"}]},{point:"Reinforcement learning and self-generated data are recast as engines that let models improve their own reasoning, self-correction and reasoning-generalist training bootstrap capability from a model's own outputs.",papers:[{ay:"Kumar 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.12917"},{ay:"PENG 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.02108"},{ay:"Luo 2023",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2308.09583"}]},{point:"Generative modeling converges on unifying structural frameworks that dissolve the boundary between autoregression and diffusion and align representation with generation, replacing ad hoc recipes with general processes.",papers:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Holderrieth 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.20587"},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940"}]},{point:"These structured, flow- and scaling-based methods transfer out of language into the physical sciences and robotics, protein design, fluid simulation, imitation learning, and brain-like models all inherit the same machinery.",papers:[{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710"},{ay:"Valencia 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.02843"},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18647"},{ay:"Rathi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11516"}]}],counter_evidence:[{point:"The evaluation apparatus that would confirm this progress is itself confounded and gameable, training on the test task inflates emergence, null models win automatic benchmarks, contamination corrupts multimodal scores, and naive splits make results misleading.",papers:[{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07137"},{ay:"Yang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08695"},{ay:"Zhang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2504.09481"}]},{point:"Self-improvement and instruction tuning face principled ceilings, an information-theoretic sharpening bound, a persistent generation-verification gap, knowledge-entropy decay, and instruction tuning that can weaken context reliance.",papers:[{ay:"Song 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.02674"},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Goyal 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Kim 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.01380"}]},{point:"Structural gains in capability have not made safety robust, alignment stays only a few tokens deep, benign fine-tuning re-breaks it, preference alignment can erode trustworthiness, and models still exhibit manipulative dark patterns.",papers:[{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946"},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.01586"},{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.18870"},{ay:"Kran 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.10728"}]},{point:"Our understanding of the representations underneath these systems lags the engineering, we still lack settled accounts of why semi-supervised features generalize, what cross-entropy identifies, why a modality gap persists, and how forgetting works, the very cluster the benchmark cluster does not reach.",papers:[{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11206"},{ay:"Reizinger 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21869"},{ay:"Schrodi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.07983"},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.11019"}]}],gap:"The key open gap is that the field's evaluation instruments and its theory of representation barely touch, Datasets & Benchmarks and Representation & Transfer Learning are the least-coupled school pair (weight 6.6), so claims about what makes representations transfer or persist are rarely tested on the benchmarks that now steer progress.",why_it_matters:"Until benchmarks can measure representation-level properties, the cohort's central move, structuring computation and data rather than scaling it, cannot be validated where it matters most, and gains in leaderboard behavior may keep outrunning genuine understanding of what models learn."},n=[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708",modality:"interpretability",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Studies how individual multi-head attention heads contribute to LLM safety, showing that specific heads are pivotal to safety behavior so that circumventing them can weaken the model's guardrails against harmful generations.",limitations:"Centering the analysis on attention heads may miss safety mechanisms distributed across other model components.",community:0},{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Argues current LLM safety alignment is 'shallow'-concentrated in the first few generated tokens-which lets simple attacks or even benign fine-tuning jailbreak aligned models, and calls for deepening alignment beyond those initial tokens.",limitations:"Deepening alignment may trade off against helpfulness or add training cost, which the analysis does not fully quantify.",community:0},{ay:"Kran 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.10728",modality:"benchmark",n:"660 prompts across six categories",key_findings:"Introduces DarkBench, a benchmark of 660 prompts across six categories (e.g., brand bias) for detecting manipulative 'dark' design patterns in interactions with LLMs.",limitations:"The six predefined categories may not exhaustively capture emerging manipulative behaviors.",community:0},{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes MoE++, a heterogeneous Mixture-of-Experts framework that adds zero-computation experts alongside standard feed-forward experts to improve both the effectiveness and efficiency of MoE language models.",limitations:"Reported gains depend on the routing and architecture choices used in the authors' setups.",community:1},{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Identifies 'training on the test task'-preparing models for a benchmark's task format rather than its data-as a confound that distorts LLM evaluation and inflates apparent emergent abilities.",limitations:"The confound is conceptually characterized but can be hard to disentangle in practice.",community:1},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039",modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Transfusion, which trains a single transformer combining next-token-prediction and diffusion losses over mixed discrete-text and continuous-image sequences to unify multimodal generation and understanding.",limitations:"Jointly optimizing two objectives in one model adds training complexity and modality-balancing considerations.",community:1},{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537",modality:"theory/proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that linear RNNs such as Mamba, RWKV and GLA fail at state-tracking due to a restricted eigenvalue range, and that permitting negative eigenvalues unlocks their state-tracking capability.",limitations:"Focuses on state-tracking tasks; broader effects on standard language modeling are less explored.",community:2},{ay:"MOUFAD 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09945",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes a variational diffusion posterior-sampling method with midpoint guidance for using diffusion models as priors in Bayesian inverse problems, tackling the intractable terms in the denoising posterior.",limitations:"Approximation quality and compute cost may vary across different inverse-problem settings.",community:2},{ay:"Rusch 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.03943",modality:"theory/proof",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces LinOSS, a linear oscillatory state-space model built from forced harmonic oscillators, enabling stable and efficient learning over long sequences with long-range interactions.",limitations:"The biologically inspired design is validated mainly on long-range sequence benchmarks.",community:2},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.18647",modality:"robotics",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Investigates whether data scaling laws seen in NLP and vision also hold for robotic manipulation, studying how imitation-learning generalization improves with the amount of demonstration data.",limitations:"Findings are grounded in specific manipulation tasks and data-collection setups.",community:3},{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Presents Proteina, a large-scale flow-based protein backbone generator that uses hierarchical fold-class conditioning for scalable de novo protein structure design.",limitations:"Generates backbones only; downstream sequence design and experimental validation remain separate steps.",community:3},{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.19756",modality:"interpretability",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks (KANs), which place learnable activation functions on edges rather than fixed activations on nodes, as more interpretable alternatives to MLPs for science applications.",limitations:"Benefits are demonstrated mainly at smaller scales; scaling and training efficiency versus MLPs remain open.",community:3},{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.09967",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Ctrl-Adapter, a framework that adapts pretrained ControlNets to new image and video diffusion backbones by bridging feature-space mismatches, enabling versatile spatial control across models.",limitations:"Effectiveness depends on the quality and coverage of the pretrained ControlNets being adapted.",community:4},{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.17242",modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces LVSM, a transformer-based novel view synthesis model with minimal 3D inductive bias that scalably synthesizes new views from sparse inputs using encoder-decoder or decoder-only designs.",limitations:"Reducing 3D priors may require more data or compute and could affect out-of-distribution viewpoints.",community:4},{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21264",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Presents LARP, a video tokenizer with a learned autoregressive generative prior that departs from patchwise tokenization to produce discrete tokens better suited for autoregressive video generation.",limitations:"Adding a learned prior to the tokenization stage increases pipeline complexity.",community:5},{ay:"Yu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06940",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Shows that aligning a diffusion transformer's internal representations with strong self-supervised features (REPA) makes training diffusion transformers substantially easier and improves generation quality.",limitations:"Relies on the availability of a strong external representation model to align against.",community:5},{ay:"Frans 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.12557",modality:"diffusion model",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces shortcut models that condition on the sampling step size, enabling high-quality one-step and few-step generation without the many-pass iterative denoising of standard diffusion and flow-matching models.",limitations:"Single-step samples may still trade some fidelity relative to many-step sampling.",community:5},{ay:"Fagnou 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2501.17086",modality:"optimization",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes an iterative gradient-propagation scheme along the residual path to mitigate the inherent sequentiality of backpropagation and accelerate training of very deep residual models.",limitations:"Practical speedups depend on model depth and on hardware/implementation details.",community:6},{ay:"Kim 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08633",modality:"theory/proof",n:"one-layer transformer",key_findings:"Provides a theoretical analysis showing that a one-layer transformer trained to recursively generate intermediate states (chain-of-thought) can provably and efficiently solve the parity problem.",limitations:"The analysis is restricted to a simplified one-layer model and the parity task, limiting direct generality.",community:6},{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.16184",modality:"benchmark",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces RM-Bench, a benchmark that evaluates language-model reward models on their sensitivity to subtle content differences and robustness to style, exposing weaknesses hidden by prior benchmarks.",limitations:"Benchmark scope may not cover all reward-model use cases or domains.",community:7},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877",modality:"benchmark",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Presents BigCodeBench, a benchmark that tests LLM code generation on tasks requiring diverse function calls and complex, compositional instructions beyond simple problems.",limitations:"Task construction focuses on Python function-call scenarios.",community:7},{ay:"Chan 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07095",modality:"benchmark",n:"75 Kaggle competitions",key_findings:"Introduces MLE-bench, curating 75 Kaggle machine-learning-engineering competitions into a benchmark for measuring how well AI agents perform real-world ML engineering.",limitations:"Kaggle-derived tasks may not represent all production ML engineering workflows.",community:7},{ay:"Matthews 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.23208",modality:"RL",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Introduces Kinetix, an open-ended space of physics-based 2D control tasks for training and evaluating general RL agents, probing generalization in sequential decision-making.",limitations:"Physics-based abstract tasks may not transfer directly to real-world robotics.",community:8},{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617",modality:"RL",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes Iterative Nash Policy Optimization (INPO), aligning LLMs to general (non-Bradley-Terry) preferences by framing RLHF as a no-regret game and solving for a Nash policy rather than fitting an explicit reward model.",limitations:"The game-theoretic approach still assumes access to reliable pairwise preference signals.",community:8},{ay:"Jiang 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.11019",modality:"LLM",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Uses 'function vectors' to characterize and mitigate catastrophic forgetting during continual instruction tuning of large language models.",limitations:"The function-vector analysis is demonstrated on instruction-tuning settings and specific model families.",community:9},{ay:"Pal 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.06912",modality:"vision-language",n:"not reported",key_findings:"Proposes compositional entailment learning that embeds image-text pairs in hyperbolic space to capture the natural hierarchy of visual and textual concepts, improving vision-language representation.",limitations:"Hyperbolic embedding adds geometric and optimization complexity relative to Euclidean CLIP.",community:9}],t={eras:[{period:"Wave 1 · Pretraining at scale",title:"The Scaling Inheritance",dominant_question:"Can capability still be bought with more parameters, more data, and better-curated corpora?",description:"The cohort opens inside the paradigm it inherited: scale the pretrained model, but do it smarter. Sparse Mixture-of-Experts is the recurring lever, from the zero-computation experts of MoE++ to the fully open OLMoE. The other half of the wave attacks data rather than parameters, treating corpus selection as an optimal-control problem and manufacturing extra signal through synthetic continued pretraining. Together these papers accept scaling as the engine while trying to raise its efficiency-per-token.",key_papers:[{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Muennighoff 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.02060"},{ay:"Yang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.07431"},{ay:"Gu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07064"}]},{period:"Wave 2 · The efficiency squeeze",title:"Doing More With Less",dominant_question:"Once models are enormous, how do we make training and inference cheap enough to use?",description:"A parallel current treats efficiency itself as the research object. On the language side, MoDeGPT compresses via modular matrix decomposition, Cut Your Losses tames the memory blow-up of large-vocabulary training, FlexPrefill sparsifies attention for long contexts, and Judge Decoding speeds speculative sampling. On the generative side, continuous-time consistency models collapse many denoising steps into few. The shared conviction is that the next gains come from the inference and memory budget, not the parameter count.",key_papers:[{ay:"Lin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.09632"},{ay:"Wijmans 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.09009"},{ay:"Lai 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2502.20766"},{ay:"Bachmann 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2501.19309"},{ay:"Lu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.11081"}]},{period:"Wave 3 · Models that teach themselves",title:"The Reasoning Turn",dominant_question:"Can a model improve its own reasoning without new human labels, and where does that stop?",description:"Reasoning becomes the cohort's obsession, and the strategy shifts from scaling knowledge to eliciting it. WizardMath bootstraps mathematical skill through reinforced instruction evolution, RL teaches models to self-correct, and ReGenesis grows reasoning generalists from self-generated trajectories, while others argue that allocating compute at test time can beat adding parameters. A theoretical undercurrent runs alongside the enthusiasm: chain-of-thought is shown to let transformers provably solve parity, and the 'sharpening' analysis formalizes what self-improvement can and cannot create. The wave is optimistic about self-improvement but already reaching for its guardrails.",key_papers:[{ay:"Luo 2023",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2308.09583"},{ay:"Kumar 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.12917"},{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Kim 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.08633"}]},{period:"Wave 4 · One recipe for everything",title:"Generative Convergence",dominant_question:"Is there a single generative principle beneath tokens, pixels, and molecules?",description:"The historical split between autoregression and diffusion begins to dissolve. Transfusion trains one transformer with both a next-token loss and a diffusion loss over mixed-modality sequences; Block Diffusion literally interpolates between the two families; LARP pushes learned autoregressive tokenizers for video while Generator Matching frames generation over arbitrary Markov processes. The same flow-and-diffusion machinery reappears in the sciences, where Proteina scales flow-based protein backbone generation. Generation stops being a set of rival tricks and starts to look like one unifying language.",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21264"},{ay:"Holderrieth 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.20587"},{ay:"Geffner 2025",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2503.00710"}]},{period:"Wave 5 · Looking inside",title:"Opening the Black Box",dominant_question:"Can we see inside these models well enough to trust, edit, and secure them?",description:"As capability outruns understanding, a large fraction of the cohort turns inward. Sparse autoencoders are scaled and evaluated to pull interpretable features out of superposition, and the geometry of categorical and hierarchical concepts is mapped as linear structure. Mechanistic work localizes function to specific attention heads and sparse feature circuits, while safety papers show that alignment lodged in only the first few tokens is shallow and easily undone. Interpretability, attribution, and safety merge into a single project: making the internals legible enough to act on.",key_papers:[{ay:"Gao 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.04093"},{ay:"Marks 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2403.19647"},{ay:"Park 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.01506"},{ay:"Qi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.05946"},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708"}]},{period:"Wave 6 · From models to agents",title:"The Agentic Frontier and the Evaluation Reckoning",dominant_question:"If models are becoming agents, how do we measure what they can actually do?",description:"The final current pushes models out into the world as agents and, in the same breath, confronts the crisis of how to grade them. New benchmarks target realistic labor: machine-learning engineering, code with complex tool calls, cybersecurity, and universal visual grounding for GUI agents. But a skeptical countercurrent shows the ground is unstable, arguing that training on the test task confounds claims of emergence and that even null models can top automatic leaderboards. Benchmark-building and benchmark-doubt advance together, leaving evaluation itself as an open frontier.",key_papers:[{ay:"Chan 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07095"},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877"},{ay:"Gou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.05243"},{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Dorner 2025",doi:null}]}],debates:[{name:"Autoregression vs. Diffusion for Generation",side_a:"Next-token autoregression is the universal substrate: extend it to images, video, and beyond with better learned tokenizers and guidance-free alignment.",side_b:"Diffusion and flow are a better fit for continuous data and offer parallel, controllable generation; the future is diffusion-native or an explicit hybrid.",description:"This is the cohort's most visible fault line, and its papers keep trying to straddle it. Transfusion fuses next-token prediction with diffusion inside one transformer, and Block Diffusion is designed to interpolate between the two regimes outright. LARP defends the autoregressive route by learning video tokenizers, while Guidance-Free AR removes classifier-free guidance to make AR generation cleaner; Generator Matching goes further and subsumes both under arbitrary Markov processes.",status:"Converging rather than resolved: the dominant move in this cohort is to hybridize or unify the two families instead of declaring a winner.",key_papers:[{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2408.11039"},{ay:"Arriola 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Wang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.21264"},{ay:"Chen 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.09347"},{ay:"Holderrieth 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.20587"}]},{name:"Scale the Same Architecture vs. Change the Architecture",side_a:"Keep scaling the transformer: sparse Mixture-of-Experts and better data and precision deliver capability predictably and cheaply.",side_b:"Returns are thinning; spend compute at inference instead, or replace the core primitives with Kolmogorov-Arnold networks, linear RNNs/state-space models, or synthesized architectures.",description:"One camp, exemplified by MoE++, treats architecture as fixed and pours effort into sparse scaling and efficiency. The other questions the substrate itself: KAN swaps fixed-node activations for learnable edge functions, linear-RNN work unlocks state-tracking as a transformer alternative, and STAR automatically synthesizes tailored architectures. Cutting across both, the test-time-compute argument claims inference-time search can be more effective than adding parameters at all.",status:"Live and unsettled: scaling remains the default, but architectural alternatives and test-time compute are the fastest-growing challengers in the cohort.",key_papers:[{ay:"Jin 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07348"},{ay:"Snell 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.19756"},{ay:"Grazzi 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.12537"},{ay:"Thomas 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2411.17800"}]},{name:"Can Models Bootstrap Themselves?",side_a:"Yes: models can manufacture their own training signal through synthetic data, self-correction, and self-reward, growing into reasoning generalists without external supervision.",side_b:"No free lunch: self-improvement can only sharpen what a model already knows, not add information, and naive instruction tuning can even erode context reliance.",description:"ReGenesis and RL-based self-correction embody the optimistic view that models can iterate on their own outputs, and synthetic continued pretraining stretches limited data. The skeptical papers supply the counterweight: the sharpening analysis frames self-improvement as amplification rather than creation, 'Mind the Gap' probes where the loop stalls, and Context-Parametric Inversion shows instruction finetuning can backfire on context use. The disagreement is less about whether self-improvement works than about its ceiling.",status:"Contested but narrowing: enthusiasm is increasingly bounded by formal limits, with consensus forming that self-improvement amplifies existing capability rather than conjuring new information.",key_papers:[{ay:"PENG 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.02108"},{ay:"Kumar 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2409.12917"},{ay:"Huang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.01951"},{ay:"Song 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2412.02674"},{ay:"Goyal 2025",doi:null}]},{name:"Interpretability: Linear Features vs. Causal Circuits",side_a:"Concepts live in linear directions: scale sparse autoencoders and read features off the residual stream, using the geometry of concepts as the unit of explanation.",side_b:"Understanding requires causal mechanism: trace sparse feature circuits, identify the roles of specific attention and retrieval heads, and edit knowledge in weight null-spaces.",description:"The feature camp scales sparse autoencoders and charts the geometry of categorical and hierarchical concepts, betting that meaning is linearly encoded. The mechanism camp insists on causal wiring, discovering sparse feature circuits, dissecting the attention heads responsible for safety, and editing facts through null-space-constrained updates. In practice the two are increasingly braided, with feature dictionaries feeding circuit-level and safety analyses.",status:"Complementary and merging: the frontier stitches interpretable features into causal circuits rather than choosing between them.",key_papers:[{ay:"Gao 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.04093"},{ay:"Park 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.01506"},{ay:"Marks 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2403.19647"},{ay:"Zhou 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.13708"},{ay:"Fang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.02355"}]},{name:"Benchmark-Driven vs. Contamination-Aware Evaluation",side_a:"Progress is measured by ever harder, more realistic benchmarks: agentic ML engineering, tool-rich code, cybersecurity, and enterprise-grade tasks.",side_b:"Benchmarks are compromised: training on the test task inflates apparent emergence, null models game leaderboards, and static suites leak, so evaluation needs theory and contamination-robust design.",description:"MLE-bench and BigCodeBench push toward realistic, high-stakes tasks, treating harder benchmarks as the engine of progress. The skeptics show how fragile that signal is: training on the test task confounds emergence claims, null models achieve high win rates on automatic benchmarks, and formal limits bound what an LLM judge can certify. Dynamic and similarity-aware evaluation schemes try to repair the measurement itself.",status:"An active reckoning: benchmark construction and benchmark skepticism are advancing in tandem, with evaluation methodology now a research frontier in its own right.",key_papers:[{ay:"Chan 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07095"},{ay:"Zhuo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2406.15877"},{ay:"Dominguez-Olmedo 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.07890"},{ay:"Zheng 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.07137"},{ay:"Dorner 2025",doi:null}]},{name:"Reward-Based RLHF vs. Preference-Game Alignment",side_a:"Scalar reward models trained under the Bradley-Terry assumption, optimized with RLHF, remain the workhorse for aligning models to human preference.",side_b:"Scalar rewards are too lossy: move to general-preference and Nash game-theoretic optimization, direct preference judgment, or inference-time control, and question whether more RLHF even yields more trust.",description:"RM-Bench stress-tests reward models on subtlety and style while a rethink of reward modeling probes the Bradley-Terry premise directly. The challengers reformulate alignment as a no-regret game over general preferences and annotate preferences directly to bypass scalar reward learning. The stakes are sharpened by the finding that more preference alignment does not straightforwardly translate into more trustworthiness.",status:"Reward models still standard but actively challenged: preference-game and direct-judgment methods are the rising alternatives in this cohort.",key_papers:[{ay:"Liu 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2410.16184"},{ay:"Zhang 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2407.00617"},{ay:"Li 2024",doi:"10.48550/arxiv.2404.18870"},{ay:"Sun 2025",doi:null},{ay:"Kim 2025",doi:null}]}]},r=`## Where the field stood, in one map

The 213 Oral papers of ICLR 2025 are the conference's own judgment about what mattered most a year ago, and read together they behave less like ten disciplines than like one long argument. The official primary-area topics sort them into ten "schools" — from *Alignment, Safety & Interpretability* (the largest, 31 papers) down to *Representation & Transfer Learning* (13) — but the community structure is deliberately weak: a modularity of roughly 0.16 over an average of nearly fifteen coupling links per paper. What holds the map together is not a topic but a substrate. Almost every cluster is doing something to, with, or about the transformer, and the references they share are largely the same canon. A year on, that shared substrate is legible in accumulated influence: the application-heavy schools — *Science, Robotics & Systems* and *Multimodal Applications* — already average around fifty citations per paper, while the theory-first clusters sit near one or two.

## The center of gravity: alignment meets the base model

The single strongest coupling in the whole cohort runs between *Alignment, Safety & Interpretability* and *Foundation Models / LLMs* — a shared-reference weight (~404) that dwarfs every other pair. This is the cohort's spine. On the foundation-model side sit the engineering landmarks: mixture-of-experts efficiency in MoE++ (**Jin 2024**) and the open OLMoE (**Muennighoff 2024**), synthetic data as a pretraining lever (**Yang 2024**), and the early scaffolding of the reasoning era — self-correction trained by RL (**Kumar 2024**) and test-time compute traded against parameters (**Snell 2025**). The alignment side reads the same base-model literature but turns it inward: where safety actually lives in the network (**Zhou 2024**, attention heads), how shallow current alignment really is (**Qi 2024**), and how to see inside at all — sparse autoencoders at scale (**Gao 2024**), sparse feature circuits (**Marks 2024**), and null-space knowledge editing (**Fang 2024**). Interpretability and capability were not separate agendas here; they were citing each other.

## The generative and scientific frontier

The next-strongest bonds are generative. *Science, Robotics & Systems* couples tightly to *Generative Models* (~250), and *Generative Models* in turn to both *Foundation Models* and *Multimodal Applications*. The through-line is the diffusion-and-flow toolkit escaping image synthesis. Within the generative school it is being sharpened — video tokenization in LARP (**Wang 2024**), representation-aligned diffusion training (**Yu 2024**), faster sampling via consistency models (**Lu 2024**) and shortcut models (**Frans 2024**), and the autoregressive-diffusion hybrid of Block Diffusion (**Arriola 2025**). Next door, the same machinery does science: flow-based protein backbones in Proteina (**Geffner 2025**) and data-scaling laws for robot imitation (**Lin 2024**). The two most-cited Orals of the entire cohort live on this frontier — KAN (**Liu 2024**), an interpretable alternative to the MLP, and SAM 2 (**Ravi 2024**), promptable segmentation for images and video — evidence that a year later the field rewarded broadly usable primitives most of all.

## The bridge papers

If coupling weight shows the mass, betweenness shows the connective tissue — and it lands on a surprising set of papers. The highest-betweenness node is not a frontier model but TopoLM (**Rathi 2024**), a topographic language model with brain-like spatial organization, wiring neuroscience into the LLM cluster. Close behind are two pieces of transformer theory: residual-path gradient propagation for faster training (**Fagnou 2025**) and the mean-field account of meta-stable clustering inside attention (**Bruno 2024**). Then the RL-inflected self-correction work (**Kumar 2024**), and a study of machine creativity by attributing model text against the web (**Lu 2025**). The pattern is telling: what stitches the cohort together is disproportionately the "why does the stack work" literature — theory, mechanistic interpretability, and brain-inspired modeling — rather than the headline systems themselves.

## The gap

Not everything talks. The weakest coupling of any pair falls between *Datasets & Benchmarks* and *Representation & Transfer Learning* — a shared-reference weight near seven, roughly sixty times thinner than the alignment–foundation spine. The benchmark school is outward-facing and frontier-anchored: code generation in BigCodeBench (**Zhuo 2024**), reward-model probing in RM-Bench (**Liu 2024**), interleaved multimodal evaluation (**Xia 2024**), contextual theorem proving (**Hu 2024**). The representation school is inward and theoretical: why FixMatch generalizes (**Li 2024**), hyperbolic vision-language geometry (**Pal 2024**), Kuramoto oscillatory neurons (**Miyato 2024**), grid-cell conformal isometry (**Xu 2024**). They cite different lineages of the literature and barely meet — as, similarly, do *Multimodal Applications* and *Reinforcement Learning*. These are the seams where the shared substrate runs out.

## What it set in motion

Read forward, the ICLR 2025 Orals look like a field consolidating around one architecture while nervously auditing it. The reasoning turn is already latent here in self-correction and test-time compute; the generative unification of diffusion and flow is spreading from pixels into proteins and policies; and mechanistic interpretability has moved from curiosity to load-bearing infrastructure. The low modularity is the real headline: ten nominal schools, one conversation, everything routed through the transformer — and the papers doing the most to connect them were the ones asking why it works at all.`,o={schools:e,gap:i,argument_spine:a,evidence:n,debate:t,review_markdown:r};export{a as argument_spine,t as debate,o as default,n as evidence,i as gap,r as review_markdown,e as schools};
